<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Palatinate-Zweibrücken</h1><p class="st-meta">1453 CE – 1797 CE · capital Zweibrücken</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Wittelsbach duchy in the western Palatinate whose family gave Sweden three kings and Bavaria its first king.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1453 the lands of Duke Stephen of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-simmern">Palatinate-Simmern</a> were divided, and his son Louis I the Black received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-veldenz">Veldenz</a>, founding the Palatinate-Zweibrücken line. Louis spent much of his reign in conflict with his cousins of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled scattered lands in the western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-count-palatine-of-zweibrucken">Wolfgang</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and founded the Hornbach school in 1559, while John I later adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>. A cadet branch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-kleeburg">Palatinate-Kleeburg</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> three kings, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> in 1654, and from 1681 to 1718 Zweibrücken itself was ruled by the kings of Sweden.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1793 French armies occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed it in 1797. Its duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Maximilian Joseph</a> inherited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a> in 1799 and in 1806 became the first king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>, whose royal house descended from Zweibrücken.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palatinate-zweibrucken.jpg" alt="Palatinate-Zweibrücken" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Map_of_Pfalz-Zweibrücken_(1700)-NL.svg: Sir Iain derivative work: Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Pfalz-Zweibr%C3%BCcken_(1700)-de.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1453 CE – 1797 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zweibrücken</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wolfgang, Charles XI of Sweden, Maximilian Joseph</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pfalz-Zweibrücken, Duchy of Zweibrücken</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1625&amp;focus=palatinate-zweibrucken" aria-label="Palatinate-Zweibrücken on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 460 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palatinate-Zweibrücken lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5170 716L5229 694M5170 716L5381 440M5170 716L5054 728M5170 716L5161 697M5170 716L5165 704M5170 716L5161 697"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5381 440h0M5054 728h0M5161 697h0M5165 704h0M5161 697h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5170 716h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5170 716h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1625&amp;focus=palatinate-zweibrucken">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
